  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "👽 Alien Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for four kinds of alien, each with its own sound: K'rrak, Ulawae, Hums-at-Dawn, Velion."
    },
    "intro": "The best alien names sound like they come from a real language: one with its own sounds, which a human mouth can only nearly manage. This **alien name generator** gives each kind of alien its own sound, from the clicks of a hive species to the soft vowels of an ocean world, and can add a role and a home.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How science fiction names its aliens",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Give a species its own sounds.</strong> Star Trek's Klingon was built as a real language by the linguist Marc Okrand, full of harsh sounds English rarely uses. Vulcan names follow a pattern too: men's often begin with S (Spock, Sarek), women's with T' (T'Pol).</li><li><strong>The apostrophe</strong> usually stands for a sound English doesn't write: a click, or a catch in the throat. Use it consistently and it means something; scatter it and it's decoration.</li><li><strong>Names humans can't say.</strong> Lovecraft called Cthulhu's name a human attempt at sounds no human throat could make. Some aliens in fiction just give humans a nickname to use instead.</li><li><strong>Names that are descriptions.</strong> Some fictional species name themselves with phrases, like a translated title. It's an easy way to make an alien feel truly different.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Four kinds of alien",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Insectoid or hive</strong>: hard clicks and buzzes, with an apostrophe for the click. <em>K'rrak</em>, <em>Ts'zek</em>.</li><li><strong>Aquatic</strong>: open vowels and liquid sounds, as if spoken underwater. <em>Ulawae</em>, <em>Lioloa</em>.</li><li><strong>Energy or crystal</strong>: beings who name themselves by what they do. <em>Hums-at-Dawn</em>, <em>Folds-Through-Glass</em>.</li><li><strong>Galactic empire</strong>: long, grand names for admirals and consuls. <em>Velion</em>, <em>Sarianthe</em>.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Building a species",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Pick a sound and stick to it.</strong> Every member of a species should sound related. Generate a batch, keep the ones that feel like one language, and bin the rest.</li><li><strong>Let the name show the body.</strong> A species with mandibles can't say <em>m</em> or <em>b</em>; a species that sings might have names in two tones.</li><li><strong>Give them a word for us.</strong> What an alien species calls humans says more about them than anything else.</li><li><strong>Give them a ship and a crew.</strong> Name their vessels with the <a href=\"spaceship-name-generator.html\">Spaceship Name Generator</a>, their machines with the <a href=\"robot-name-generator.html\">Robot Name Generator</a>, and the humans they meet with the <a href=\"cyberpunk-name-generator.html\">Cyberpunk Name Generator</a>.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What makes a good alien name?",
        "answer": "Sounds that go together and don't sound like English, used consistently across a species. Short, odd names are easier for readers to remember than long ones."
      },
      {
        "question": "Why do alien names have apostrophes?",
        "answer": "In science fiction an apostrophe usually marks a sound English doesn't write, like a click or a glottal stop. Here, the insectoid names use it that way."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names in my game or story?",
        "answer": "Yes, they're free to use and you don't need to credit the site."
      }
    ]
  }
}
